 36MG c  Compiled by E. J. Gass (NNDC,BNL) (February 28, 2023).                 
 36MG c  {+48}Ca beam was accelerated to 172.3 MeV/u through the FRIB linac.    
 36MG2c  Fully-stripped 120 particle-nA beam of {+48}Ca{+20+} collided with     
 36MG3c  8.89mm {+9}Be fragmentation target. Preseparator selected nuclei with  
 36MG4c  B|r of 5.100 Tm to optimize {+42}Si. Additional separation was         
 36MG5c  carried out by the ARIS. The cocktail beam of fragments was delivered  
 36MG6c  to FDSi at 4.904 Tm. In the center of FDSi was a 5 mm-thick            
 36MG7c  fast-response YSO scintillation detector sandwiched by two plastic     
 36MG8c  scintillator veto detectors. This was surrounded by 11 HPGe clover     
 36MG9c  detectors and 15 fast-timing LaBr{-3} detectors in a hemisphere to     
 36MGac  the north. On the south side were 88 neutron detectors of the VANDLE   
 36MGbc  array in two layers 90 and 96 cm from the implantation detector. Two   
 36MGcc  large-area Si PIN detectors and a 2-mm-thick plastic scintillator      
 36MGdc  were positioned 1.5 m upstream in the last diagnostic detector box of  
 36MGec  the beamline. Ions were identified using event-by-event energy loss    
 36MGfc  in the PIN detector (to determine Z) and time-of-flight from a         
 36MGgc  plastic timing scintillator in ARIS to the timing scintillator in the  
 36MGhc  diagnostic detector box (to determine A/Q). Half-lives were obtained   
 36MGic  by fitting decay curves to a function considering the decay of the     
 36MGjc  parent, growth and decay of daughters, potential |bn and |b2n decay    
 36MGkc  branches, and constant background.                                     
